ZzzCharacter.cpp's per-frame effect switch, the MODEL_BULL_FIGHTER case:

    if (o->CurrentAction == MONSTER01_STOP1 && (o->AnimationFrame >= 15.f && o->AnimationFrame <= 20.f)) Smoke = true;
    if (o->CurrentAction == MONSTER01_STOP2 && (o->AnimationFrame >= 20.f && o->AnimationFrame <= 25.f)) Smoke = true;
    if (o->CurrentAction == MONSTER01_WALK && ((o->AnimationFrame >= 2.f && o->AnimationFrame <= 3.f) || (o->AnimationFrame >= 5.f && o->AnimationFrame <= 6.f))) Smoke = true;
    if (Smoke && rand_fps_check(2))
    {
        Vector(0.f, -4.f, 0.f, p);
        b->TransformPosition(o->BoneTransform[24], p, Position, true);
        CreateParticle(BITMAP_SMOKE, Position, o->Angle, o->Light);
    }

Bone 24 is smok_bone, a child of the head, and the name says what the artist meant: it is the muzzle, not a hoof. LEGACY's reading called this hoof dust from the frame windows alone and its own rig note corrected it - 'beside the eyes at 22 and 23, so this is the muzzle. It reads as hoof dust from the frame windows alone, and it is a snort.' The windows are keys of the action - 15 to 20 of the 20-key first idle, 20 to 25 of the 30-key second, and two short ones in the seven-key walk - and the index turns them into fractions of the clip, as it does the dragon's through_key. Four units down the bone, which is 4 cm, and every second reference frame while a window is open.

BITMAP_SMOKE with no subtype is smoke01.jpg and subtype 0: 16 frames of life, born at 0.48 to 0.80 of a 64-pixel sheet, growing 0.05 a frame, lifting on an accelerating Gravity of 0.2 a frame, fading on Luminosity = LifeTime / 8 - so it is at full brightness for the first half and gone by the end - and drawn with the ordinary alpha blend, not the additive one the fire uses. A JPEG with no alpha, so the sheet's own black is what cuts it. See client/core/Snort.cs.

No dust: this case ends in a break, so it does not fall into the Dark Knight's ground dust the way the Budge Dragon does.
